\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Rates, Rebates and the State:\\ Railway Regulation Reconsidered}
\author{H. Whitmore}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
The standard account of late nineteenth-century railway
regulation treats the rate wars of the 1880s as a straight
contest between carriers and shippers. In that account the
trunk lines alternated between open price wars and uneasy
truces, and the regulator stepped in only when the fighting
threatened the capital market. The rebate files tell a
different story: the visible wars were largely fought over
secret concessions to favoured shippers \cite{harcourt2018}.

\section{The commission records}
The commission minute books survive almost complete for the
years 1881--1902, and they record far more hearings about
rebates than about published tariffs. As \cite{webb1963}
first observed, the commissioners understood the rebate
system perfectly well and chose to police its excesses rather
than the practice itself. What the minutes do not record is
how shippers learned which concessions to ask for.

\section{Rebates as governance}
The most suggestive reading of the rebate files treats them
as a form of private governance rather than as simple price
cutting \cite{harcourt2018}. On that reading, a rebate was a
negotiated instrument that bound shipper and carrier into a
durable relationship, and the commission's tolerance of the
system amounted to a delegation of regulatory authority to
the trade itself.

\section{Conclusion}
Neither the rate-war narrative nor the commission's own
record captures what the rebates were doing. The regulation
of the railways was made inside the trade as much as it was
imposed upon it.

